    <!-- T-W2-I4-BT: Bluetooth HAL excision grace layer.
         The BT HAL feature-permission prebuilts
         (android.hardware.bluetooth.prebuilt.xml,
         android.hardware.bluetooth_le.prebuilt.xml) are dropped in
         feature-excised/bt-excised.mk, so hasSystemFeature(FEATURE_BLUETOOTH)
         and FEATURE_BLUETOOTH_LE return false and the BluetoothAdapter is
         never created. Settings' Bluetooth/ConnectedDevice controllers hide
         all BT entry points at runtime (they gate on the adapter being
         available, which requires the feature). No Settings-specific
         config_bluetooth boolean exists to override (verified: packages/apps/
         Settings gates BT UI on the BluetoothAdapter / FEATURE_BLUETOOTH, not
         a config bool). This entry documents the contract; the real hiding is
         the runtime feature/adapter check. -->

    <!-- T-BT-UI-SWEEP (FR-F §9): residual BT UI in Settings — audit.
         After the Wave-3 HAL/feature-XML excision, residual BT UI in Settings
         was audited (grep -rn "bluetooth\|bt_" packages/apps/Settings/res/xml/).
         Every BT preference/fragment is feature-gated on FEATURE_BLUETOOTH or
         on BluetoothAdapter.getDefaultAdapter() returning non-null, so they
         ALL auto-hide once the feature is absent. No Settings overlay boolean
         exists to override any of them (verified — Settings does not gate BT
         UI on a config_* bool). No resource edit is therefore needed; this
         block documents the auto-hidden items (Law 8 determinism / Law 19
         documentation completeness). Items:

         1. connected_devices.xml — `bt_nearby_slice`, `add_bt_devices`
            (BluetoothPairingDetail fragment), `bluetooth_off_footer`
            (DiscoverableFooterPreferenceController): gated via
            AddDevicePreferenceController.getAvailabilityStatus():80,
            ConnectedDeviceGroupController.getAvailabilityStatus():197,299,
            DiscoverableFooterPreferenceController.getAvailabilityStatus():76
            — all return UNSUPPORTED_ON_DEVICE when
            hasSystemFeature(FEATURE_BLUETOOTH) is false.

         2. exploit_protection_settings.xml — `bluetooth_auto_off`
            (BluetoothAutoOffPrefController): getAvailabilityStatus():21-28
            returns UNSUPPORTED_ON_DEVICE when hasSystemFeature(
            FEATURE_BLUETOOTH) is false.

         3. tether_prefs.xml — `enable_bluetooth_tethering`
            (BluetoothTetherSwitchPreference): isAvailable():58-63 returns
            false when BluetoothAdapter.getDefaultAdapter() is null.

         4. accessibility_hearing_aids.xml — `hearing_device_add_bt_devices`
            (AvailableHearingDevicePreferenceController) and saved-device
            controller: parent BaseBluetoothDevicePreferenceController
            .getAvailabilityStatus():44-45 returns UNSUPPORTED_ON_DEVICE when
            hasSystemFeature(FEATURE_BLUETOOTH) is false. The fragment page
            is also de-indexed from search via AccessibilityHearingAidsFragment
            .isPageSearchEnabled() → HearingAidHelper.isHearingAidSupported(),
            which NPE-guards on mBluetoothAdapter == null
            (HearingAidHelper.java:61-62, 94-98) — the search-index path
            returns false (page not indexed) once the adapter is null.

         5. hearing_device_pairing_detail.xml /
            bluetooth_device_details_fragment.xml /
            snoop_logger_filters_settings.xml — all reached only via the
            connected-devices or developer-options entry points above, which
            are already hidden. SnoopLoggerFiltersPreferenceController
            .getAvailabilityStatus():45-53 additionally returns
            DISABLED_DEPENDENT_SETTING unless BluetoothProperties.snoop_log_mode
            is FILTERED (default DISABLED), so the snoop-logs BT group is
            hidden regardless of the BT feature.

         6. location_settings.xml — `ic_bluetooth_more_vert` is a drawable
            NAME only (a "more" vertical-dots icon that happens to share the
            bluetooth_ prefix); it is not BT UI and is intentionally NOT
            touched (Law 6).

         Restore-from-backup (§11): Settings preferences do not carry backup
         metadata that could re-instate a hidden BT preference — Settings
         backup is keyed on the preference XML, and the controllers' runtime
         feature check runs after every restore, so a restored preference row
         is filtered out by getAvailabilityStatus() on the next attach. -->


    <!-- T-W2-I5-LOC: GNSS HAL excision grace layer (location-only).
         The GPS feature-permission prebuilt
         (android.hardware.location.gps.prebuilt.xml) is dropped in
